{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1795120426,"nonce":"II8c/A6h7uTYsQF5CV1q6N9L/aQ=","title":"Log markers: an author can print a marker line to grow or confuse the stored log","message":"Found in the batch/2026-11-19 round-3 review (scratchpad round3-reviewer.md / round3-red-team.md, summarised here). R3-8/RT-R3-4/R3-9/R3-10/RT-R3-10/RT-R3-11: marker chunks are recognised by length and body, so a step that prints a marker text as a lone chunk grows its log on every retry, and a forged marker followed by *** can make a later re-read unverifiable (needs a tail break the author cannot force). A secret value that starts with *** is classed Mismatch. A clean stream end mid-secret stores its prefix. The full re-read on each retry costs O(stored log). Fix: store markers out of band (a chunk flag in the index) instead of in the body.","files":null}]}